// #110 R1 — `return t.0;` dropped the element TWICE. The return path's move
// walker was a LOCAL LAMBDA in lower_return, a hand copy of the member
// `mark_moved_in_expr_recursive`; the member had grown a TupleIndex case and
// the copy had not, so nothing marked `t.0` moved and the tuple's scope-exit
// SDrop freed the element the returned value already owned. The two walkers
// are now one.
//
// Concrete carrier, no enum, no generics — this is NOT the auto-Copy root that
// the rest of #110 closed; it reproduces on a struct whose field has its own
// `impl Drop`. Its control twin (drop_tuple_element_returned_once_control)
// spells the same move through a LOCAL first, the path that was already
// correct: the pair says the difference is the direct-return position.
//
// ⚠ Exactly one line. Two is the defect; zero would be a suppressed destructor.
package droptupret;
use logos.std.fmt;
struct Inner { pub n: i64 }
impl Drop for Inner { fn drop(self: &mut Inner) { println!("DROP n={}", self.n); } }
struct W { pub i: Inner }
fn prod() -> W { let t: (W, i64) = (W { i: Inner { n: 7i64 } }, 1i64); return t.0; }
fn main() -> i32 { let r: W = prod(); println!("m"); return 0i32; }
